  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A consolidation of surface lows is expected to \noccur along an elongated baroclinic zone off the U.S. East Coast\nover the next 24 hours or so. Rapid deepening of a consolidated\narea of low pressure is then forecast later Wednesday into \nThursday as a notable coastal low takes shape. This low is then \nforecast to move north towards Southern New England, then \neventually weaken as it potentially wobbles around the New \nEngland coast over the weekend. Regarding the track and the \nstrength of the low, most ensemble and deterministic guidance \nhave shown a slight trend west, and have also generally trended \ndeeper. Additionally, some guidance, particularly machine \nlearning guidance, have trended a bit slower with the northward \nprogression as well. For ENC, these trends could spell a longer \nduration of marine and coastal impacts, and potentially a slight\nuptick in the magnitude of impacts as well. Not all guidance \nhave shown the westward shift, though, and this is something \nwe'll continue to closely monitor moving forward.\n\nRegarding sensible weather, showers and isolated thunderstorms \nare expected to persist through around sunset this evening \nacross the ENC coastal plain, with periods of light rain closer \nto the coast. This showery type of pattern looks to continue \nthrough Thursday. While an area of focused lift and higher \nrainfall totals may still develop, the latest guidance have \nbacked off on this potential, and it appears the risk of \nflooding due to rain is lower than previously thought.\n\nBy far, the greatest impacts with this coastal low are expected\nfor the Outer Banks. There, a period of moderate to strong \nnortheast to north winds are expected to develop as early as \nWednesday, and then potentially last well into the day Friday, \nespecially if the latest westward and slower trend holds. \nProbabilistic guidance shows a strong signal for reaching Wind \nAdvisory criteria with both sustained winds and gusts, and and \nadvisory has been issued for Dare County, Ocracoke, and Downeast\nCarteret County. For the OBX from Cape Hatteras north, peak \ngusts could top out around 50 mph, and this may lead to some \nminor tree and power line damage, along with a risk of isolated \npower outages.\n\nThe strong, and longer duration, of the winds is also expected \nto support an increased risk of coastal impacts. Building seas \nand increasing swell periods will lead to dangerous surf \nconditions in addition to a risk of beach erosion, wave runup, \nocean overwash, and minor to moderate coastal flooding. Wave \nheights in the surf zone may reach 10-15ft at 10-13s from Duck \nto Cape Hatteras, peaking on Thursday. 6-8ft+ wave heights in \nthe surf zone may last into this weekend. A High Surf Advisory \nis now in effect from this evening through Saturday evening. The\nstrength, and duration, of the swell is expected to lead to an \nincreased risk of ocean overwash and moderate coastal flooding \nfor the vulnerable stretches of the coastline along Hatteras \nIsland. Where dune structures are less vulnerable, minor coastal\nflooding will be possible.\n\nFor soundside areas adjacent to the southern Pamlico Sound, and\nthe nearby Neuse River, persistent northeasterly winds may lead\nto minor coastal flooding later Wednesday through Friday. \nEventually the winds are expected to turn more northerly, which \nmay then allow the soundside coastal flooding risk to shift to \nportions of Ocracoke and Hatteras Villages.\n\nPlease continue to stay tuned for future updates as this is a \ncomplex and longer-duration coastal low event, with subtle \nchanges in strength and path of the low leading to increased, or\ndecreased, coastal impacts.",
